<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saxony</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saxony is a name that has moved across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It first meant the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in the north-west, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and later ruled as the Duchy of Saxony. After that duchy was broken up in 1180, the name and the electoral title passed to lands on the middle Elbe, held from 1423 by the Wettins, so that today's Free State of Saxony, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, lies in the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saxony.jpg" alt="Saxony" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saxony.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Free State of Saxony is a landlocked state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and its southern and eastern boundaries form part of Germany's frontiers with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The land rises from the lowlands of the North German Plain in the north, which include the Leipzig Bay and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, to uplands along the Czech border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Some of the largest ancient monumental temples of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> stood here, dating from the fifth millennium BCE. Since the late 6th century the area was populated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>, most prominently the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, and it was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> and organized as the Sorbian March. The territory became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> by the 10th century, and the Margravate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> was founded in 985 as a frontier march. In 1423 Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> became subject to the margravate, ruled by the Wettin dynasty, creating a new and powerful state. Although its centre lay far to the southeast of the old Saxony, it came to be called Upper Saxony and then simply Saxony, while the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> lands became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p><p>In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made the Electorate of Saxony a kingdom, in exchange for military support. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> the king was restored but forced to cede the northern part of the kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, a loss of nearly 60 percent of the territory and 40 percent of the population. Frederick <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> abdicated on 13 November 1918, and Saxony became the Free State of Saxony. It was broken up under communist rule and re-established on 3 October 1990, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> is the capital of Saxony and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> its largest city. Dresden's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> cityscape and art collections, Leipzig's musical traditions and the mining heritage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> are among its best-known cultural features.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saxony</b> was then governed by Frankish counts and bishops, and Christianity was spread through new sees such as Paderborn, Bremen and Hildesheim.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Saxony</b>’s Erzgebirge silver was struck from about 1500 into some of the first large silver coins of the type later called the thaler, the name that lives on in the word dollar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg takes its name from the Hammaburg, a fortification built in the early ninth century near the meeting of the Alster and the Elbe, probably under Charlemagne or Louis the Pious, to secure the Frankish frontier in <b>Saxony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Congress of Vienna, joined 39 sovereign states and free cities: the Austrian Empire and the Kingdom of Prussia for their former imperial lands, kingdoms such as Bavaria, <b>Saxony</b>, Württemberg and Hanover, dozens of grand duchies, duchies and principalities, and the free cities of Frankfurt, Hamburg, Bremen and Lübeck.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Prokop the Great, armies of townsmen and peasants used wagon forts of chained war wagons, defended with handguns and light artillery, to defeat the crusades launched by Emperor Sigismund and the papacy, notably at Vítkov Hill in 1420 and Domažlice in 1431, and raided <b>Saxony</b>, Silesia and Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son, Elector Frederick III, wanted a royal crown to match the elevation of the Elector of <b>Saxony</b> to the Polish throne and of William of Orange to the English one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
